more people arrive at this putin in the  northern part of khartoum it's now in  its fifth day protesters here are  demanding the military abandon politics  after it took power last october the  army announced on monday it was  withdrawing from talks to form a  transitional government to pave way for  political parties to reach an agreement  but organizers of the setting known as  resistance committees are continuing  with their protests  we think the statement from the army is  just a political process and a play with  words when we say we want them to return  to the barracks it's not just about them  leading politics it's about the reform  of the security sector  ensuring that their role is clearly  defined and making sure that they don't  have access to wealth that should be  going into the government coffers  bahrain is one of many such protests in  khartoum they started after mass  protests last month left nine people  dead and hundreds injured when security  forces used tear gas and live ammunition  to disperse demonstrations protesters  here say the army's talk of withdrawal  fails to meet their demands  we want to end the military rule because  a lot of people are suffering since they  took charge three years ago nothing has  changed and even with the army saying  it's withdrawing from talks there's been  no justice yet for the protesters killed  so we're not going anywhere  the talks were being facilitated by the  un along with the african union and the  regional body igad following the army's  withdrawal the tripartite mechanism  announced it was cancelling negotiations  and will consult political parties and  stakeholders on a new format to end  sudan's political crisis  there have been varied reactions from  political parties while some have  announced they are ready to sit down and  find ways to form a transitional  government others have denounced the  army's withdrawal rejected the  possibility of further talks and called  for more protests  as the tripartite mechanism tries to  bring the parties together resistance  committees say they will work to ensure  any deal reach is accepted by protesters  resistance committees are monitoring  bodies before being executive bodies we  will support political parties to make  sure they appoint a government of  technocrats that can work for the people  and not affiliated to any political  party our demands are very clear we want  a government with competent people that  can deliver  but it's not clear when or if that  government will be formed sudan's road  to political stability still appears a  long one hiba morgan al-jazeera khartoum
 

